<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Commune of Cremona</h1><p class="st-meta">1098 CE – 1334 CE (approx.) · capital Cremona</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A rich Po valley city-republic, usually loyal to the emperors and Milan's great rival, crowned by the Torrazzo bell tower.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Cremona's citizens won rights from their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in the late 11th century, and consuls are recorded from 1098. Commanding a crossing of the Po, the city became a major river port and soon clashed with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Milan</a> and its ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crema">Crema</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Cremona was governed by consuls and later a podestà, with a strong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of merchants and cloth makers. It generally sided with the emperors: it helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> destroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crema">Crema</a> in 1160 and was a key base for Frederick II. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torrazzo-of-cremona">Torrazzo</a>, one of the tallest medieval bell towers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and the cathedral date from this period. In the 13th century Ghibelline strongmen such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buoso-da-dovara">Buoso da Dovara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uberto-pallavicino">Uberto Pallavicino</a> held power.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Faction struggles between the Cavalcabò and their rivals weakened the commune, and in 1334 it submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azzone-visconti">Azzone Visconti</a>, becoming part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-milan">Lordship of Milan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1098 CE – 1334 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.55%;width:4.70%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1098 CE – 1334 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cremona</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Buoso da Dovara, Uberto Pallavicino</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lombard, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cremona</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1216&amp;focus=commune-of-cremona" aria-label="Commune of Cremona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4918 584 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Commune of Cremona lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5238 840L5218 830"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5238 840h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5238 840h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cremona. <a href="/atlas?year=1216&amp;focus=commune-of-cremona">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

